namespace Eigen {

/** \eigenManualPage TopicPassingByValue On the Folly of Sending Eigen Objects Abroad by Value

It is a circumstance not generally known to the casual traveller in C++, though painfully familiar to the experienced one, that to dispatch an object by value is almost always a very bad idea; for it means that a perfectly good copy must be made, at the expense of the public purse, merely that the function may look upon it for a moment and throw it away, and the prudent practitioner will therefore send a reference instead, which costs nothing and arrives in the same condition in which it set out.

\note If the gentle reader is so fortunate as to be compiling in C++17 mode with a sufficiently recent compiler (GCC >= 7, Clang >= 5, MSVC >= 19.12),
then the alignment difficulties described below are taken in hand by the compiler, as a good clerk takes in hand the business of an absent partner, and passing %Eigen objects by value
is safe (though still less efficient than passing by const reference, and the sentence upon the Treasury is therefore not wholly remitted).

With pre-C++17 compilers, however, the case is far graver; for passing \ref TopicFixedSizeVectorizable "fixed-size vectorizable Eigen objects" by value is not only inefficient, it can be illegal or make your program crash! And the reason is this: that these %Eigen objects are gentlemen of exacting habits, who bear alignment modifiers upon their persons as other gentlemen bear a crest, and that these modifiers are not respected, nay, are not so much as noticed, when the gentlemen are sent abroad by value.

Let us suppose, by way of example, a function like this, where \c v is passed by value:

\code
void my_function(Eigen::Vector2d v);
\endcode

Such a function stands in urgent need of reformation, and must be rewritten as follows, passing \c v by const reference:

\code
void my_function(const Eigen::Vector2d& v);
\endcode

Likewise, if the reader keeps a class which harbors an %Eigen object as a member (and what household is without one?):

\code
struct Foo
{
  Eigen::Vector2d v;
};
void my_function(Foo v);
\endcode

This function, too, is under the same obligation of reform, and must be rewritten like this:
\code
void my_function(const Foo& v);
\endcode

Let it be observed, on the other hand, and to the comfort of the anxious, that there is no problem whatever with functions that return objects by value; the traveller who comes home is received without difficulty, and it is only the one who goes out that is detained at the barrier.

*/

}
